For the proverbial record, I did not mean to imply that current
discussants did not have credentials, nor that those without such
credentails should not be taken seriously.  Instead, I only suggested
that in philosophical discussions, folks should "try to emulate the
cognitive style of professional philosophers".

Ed Regis writes:
>Usually, and despite appearances, nothing special hinges on such
>terms, and people pretty much know what they mean.

It seems to me that current discussions hinge crucially on what is
meant by "objective", "know", and "prove".   For example, you claim

>objective reality exists and that it's knowable through reason,
>observation, and experiment,

and you criticize 

>philosophers ... who were maintaining, even in the late 1950s, that
>atoms ... could never be proven to exist.

But until you pin down these words more, such claims need not be in
conflict.  For example, I could paraphrase these claims as:

1. There is some reality and we are not totally ignorant of it.
2. There are standards of "rationality" such that a rational person 
   could remain not totally certain about whether atoms exist.

Of course Reilly Jones claims to agree with you that your dispute does
not hinge on word meanings, so maybe the rest of you know what you
mean and I'm the exception.  If so, I'll just sit this one out.

