From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Sun, 14 Aug 94 19:08:50 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-161] EPIST: certainty again 

Ed Regis quotes me and then writes:

>>I agree with you that 100% certainty is possible, but only when I
>>keep in mind that if you can get only (for example) three
>>significant figures, 100% is the same number as (for example)
>>99.96%.  The same number, when you're talking about the real world,
>>sensations, perceptions, measurements, in short, the domain of
>>empirical science. I don't think 100.0% certainty is possible if one
>>insists on an infinite number of significant zeros after that
>>decimal point.
>
>I've come across this argument a few times and I've never bought it,
>even in the realm of number theory, where I guess it originated.  My
>problem with it is that the argument is reiterable for any number
>less than 100, so that if you keep on applying it consistently,
>pretty soon you find that there's really no difference betwen 0
>[zero] and 100, or, indeed, between any two numbers at all.

Perhaps you did not notice that the argument behind my metaphor (and
it was merely a metaphor) relied on holding the number of significant
figures constant. Clearly, if you got three significant figures out of
a measuring instrument when you measure 1.00, then that 1.00 is
definitely not the same as a measurement of 0.00. And *just* as
clearly, if you get three significant digits in a measurement of 3344,
then a measurement of 3343 is measuring the *same* quantity. The final
digits in these two measurements are noise, except insofar as they 
determine whether we should round down to 3340 or round up to 3350.

The argument did not emerge from number theory, but from the theory of
precision as developed in empirical scientific methodology.
Mathematicians have little use for numbers of less than perfect
precision, and scientists have no access to numbers of more than
imperfect precision. As far as our ability to perceive the real world,
the world of our senses, is concerned, numbers of perfect precision
are of but theoretical interest.

>If, for example, you argued that y is the same number as z because y
>approaches z asymptotically, then you could equally well argue the same thing
>regarding the next nearest number below y (say x), that as long as x
>approaches y asymptotically, then it's really the same number as y.  E.g.: 
>
>z: 100.000...
>y:  99.999...
>x:  99.998..., and so on, seriatim, back to zero, so that all numbers are
>really the same number, without excepton.  

The argument has nothing to do with "asymptotic approach". You neglect
to mention the degree of precision of your original measurement of
100.000 (that is, the number of significant figures you obtained).
Let's just say the nature of your instruments/sense-organs/whatever is
such that you got four significant digits. Then the transformations
that you show above don't destroy any significant digits; they're just
diddling the noise. When you get to the transformation you did *not*
show, namely, the move from 99.9850000.... to 99.984999..., *then* you
are altering a significant figure, destroying information, and
changing the number. You are failing to distinguish between mere
noise, on the one hand, and significant information you obtained with
your senses, on the other hand.

>it's just a way of trying to have your cake and eat it too.  Analytical
>distinctions can and should be maintained wherever they exist.   

In this case, the analytical distinction you are seeking to preserve
didn't exist in the first place. To convince me otherwise, show me a
(continuous, not countable) phenomenon which has been measured to an
infinite degree of precision. On the other hand, I'm trying to
preserve an analytical distinction which really *does* exist; the
distinction between signal and noise in measurement.

This entire subthread is subsidiary to the parts of epistemology that
I think *might* be of interest to the list, but either because I wrote
unclearly or for whatever reason, I think I've been badly
misunderstood, and I wanted to try to set things right.

