From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Sun, 14 Aug 1994 22:16:38 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-162] Individualism vs. Groupism

 sasha@cs.umb.edu (Alexander Chislenko) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-8-107:
>   Why do we view ideal societies as giving complete freedom to their
>members, and ideal 'living beings' as commanding complete control over
>their parts?   Is it just because of where we see ourselves?

 I think the view that our bodies are a hierarchical command structure
is at most a half truth. It describes nerve-muscle interactions fairly
well, but doesn't do a good job of describing hormonal control mechanisms
or the organization of the brain (see Dennett's _Consciousness Explained_
for a good attack on the theory that there is a center of consciousness).
The big difference between the organization of our bodies and social
organizations is that parts of our body know very little about how to
reorganize their relations with one another. If it were possible for
relations between parts of a body to be voluntary and changeable, I
think it would be clear that giving one part of the body complete control
over the others would be unhealthy.
 
>   If you see a distributed information system, how do you tell whether
>it is a person or a society?

 Why would I want to?
 I envision a system/world in which software agents have a continuous
range of abilities, from simple agents which just perform a sort, to
agents with enough wisdom to serve as a judge for a PPL.
 You could, in theory, apply something like a Turing test to software
agents and grant some rights only to software which passes the test.
However, I doubt that such a test can be made fast or cheap enough to
be widely used. Also, it is not obvious that any one piece of software would
have enough sophistication contained within itself to achieve intelligence;
it will more likely know how to subcontract out pieces of hard tasks, with
the result that Turing-level intelligence would not be localized (and IQ's
might fluctuate with economic conditions?).
 Why would anyone want rights to be conditioned on a definition of person-
hood such as IQ? As long as we use a criterion that is more natural for a
PPL to use (such as: can the entity complain that it's rights have been
violated? or: does the entity make and fulfill contracts?), there will be
little problems with lawsuits filed on behalf of individual organs.
 I see no disadvantages to allowing simple pieces of software to have rights
once they start contracting out their services. In fact, if they can't
acquire rights to own or rent disk space and cpu time, it will be hard
for them to acquire a reputation for reliability.

